result.strongest_argument:
Hardware is not the problem. The jobs use one GPU each, and the caps are 6.94 GPU-h central and 9.46 high. The problem is decisiveness. After the repair the screen is still unlikely to produce a verdict, and a GO it does produce can no longer be told apart from literal spill.

(1) A bundle fact the repair could have computed for free removes the condition the GO depends on. Decision 76 and the masking section say: if fewer than 40 percent of controlled unseen-stratum families have at least 32 pre-literal needle tokens, the PRE conditions are dropped at the freeze. The proposal itself says "A GO therefore rests on PRE for this channel" (proposal line 1042). I computed this fact without any model, on the development partition only, with the real Qwen3.5-4B-Base tokenizer (sha fe000e3e..., same as the lane-862 receipt) and stop lists taken from K1's FineWeb and FineWeb-2 haystack files:
- 110 controlled questions × 14 pairs = 1,540 families;
- PRE is evaluable for 19-21 percent of them under every block alignment;
- with the near rule F switched off entirely (exact matches only) it is still 29-32 percent;
- the median PRE evidence is 5-10 tokens (11-14 with exact matches only), against the 32-token minimum;
- the English-needle half reaches 14.5 percent, ja>en 13.6 percent.
So under the registration's own rule the GO condition xi^PRE >= 5 disappears, and GO rests on LF alone. S1 shows LF is moved up to +10 points by three-block spill.

I re-ran the repair's own S2 code (repair-decision-sim.py, unchanged except for an added variant that drops PRE; 1,000 reads, bootstrap 1,000). It reproduces the published "repaired" rows (P(NEG | no excess) 0.80 against 0.81; P(GO | input 12) 0.555 against 0.55). With PRE dropped:
- P(GO | no excess, long-range spill +10) = 0.225;
- P(GO | excess 5, spill +6) = 0.403;
- P(GO | real excess 12) = 0.555.
The first two are exactly wave 1's numbers, against 0.00 and 0.011 in the repaired tables. A GO therefore has a likelihood ratio of about 2.5 against long-range spill. It is not a decisive verdict.

(2) The NEGATIVE is the only verdict still identified, and its reach is overstated. S2 picks controlled questions independently of passage: 115 questions in 89 clusters. On the development text, the entity rule removes whole passages: 110 controlled questions sit in only 66 of 122 links. Re-running S2 with 66 clusters (same noise scales) gives:
- at H 30, P(NEG | no excess) of 0.75 at seed SD 1 (the proposal says 0.81);
- 0.38 at seed SD 2 (the proposal says 0.53);
- the median half-width at seed SD 2 is 4.63 against the 5.0 cap.
The seed SD is unmeasured, and no repair step can act on it: the sealed seed read only declares NEGATIVE_REACHABILITY_LOW, and the five-seed option is not adopted. sigma_star = 2.0 was chosen as the point where P(NEG) is about 0.5, but under the observed clustering it sits where P(NEG) is 0.38.

(3) The gates come first and are the likeliest stop: pre-step pass 0.6-0.8 (judgement) times H2 pass 0.42-0.57 is 0.25-0.46. The proposal already puts P(GO or NEGATIVE) at only 0.1-0.3. With GO confounded and NEGATIVE reach lower, the probability of a verdict the program could defend is about 0.05-0.2. The rest is STOP_INSTRUMENT, UNINTERPRETABLE, INCONCLUSIVE or an unattributable GO, after spending 0.5-6.4 GPU-h.

(4) The cost side is still unproven:
- no v3 code exists;
- no 4B training step has run, and the 3.06-5.05 s step is scaled from a 0.6B probe;
- the high cap is over 8 GPU-h (D24, admission is Kevin's);
- the Compute doctor is FAIL (cap 79).

Counterpoints, stated honestly:
- Mask exclusion is only 1.5 percent, better than S2's assumed 10 percent.
- LF at r=2 is evaluable for 67 percent, so r stays 2.
- GO at a real excess of 15 stays at 0.95 or more.
- 2609.15545 finds the local carrying channel in hybrids concentrated at lag one, so long-range spill may in fact be small. But the design handed exactly that question to PRE, and PRE cannot be evaluated on this data.

